WEBVTT

00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:04.790
 This is Delicious Revolution, a show about food.

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:21.640
 You know, a lot of people know about Cuba's internationalist policies in healthcare, where they educate people from all over the world, doctors and some of them out, but people don't really know that Cuba's doing that in other areas, such as sustainable agriculture.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:35.010
 There are people from hundreds of different countries that are training there, and in the agrarian university, we met people from all over the world, and everybody there was really focused on a particular aspect of agriculture that they felt,

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:39.040
 would help their communities in some way.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.040
 Very specific.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:01:00.430
 Not just going to a university and learning about farming, or learning about sustainable farming, but learning about specific agroecological, organic, as much as possible approaches to specific issues that they could bring that education back home and have an impact on, or at least work with others that can create a plan for something that would change, you know.

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:08.510
 Delicious Revolution is a show about food, culture, and place.

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:10.670
 Knitted by Chelsea Wills, and me, Devin Sampson.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:21.050
 We talk with people who work in and think about all aspects of the food movement, including farmers, chefs, artists, and activists, and who have a vision for a different food system.

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:29.870
 This first season of Delicious Revolution, we talk to friends who are deeply engaged with many aspects of food, and who have inspired us over years with their thoughts and stories.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:38.240
 You can subscribe to Delicious Revolution on iTunes, or your favorite podcast app, and you can find us at deliciousrevolutionshow.com.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:52.270
 For the two of you, I can't help but think about places and wild and cultivated places as part of your story together.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:56.160
 When did this start, and what's your fascination with that?

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:20.230
 Well, I think I would probably defer a little bit to Pete, because my journey is probably from an urban landscape, growing up in St. Louis, Missouri, coming to school at UC Santa Cruz, and sort of being introduced, in a way, to nature and outdoors and farming.

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:36.990
 That was all very new to me, I would say, and Pete and I met sort of at the end of our college experience, and at that point, Pete was already pretty into farming and had started Sand Hill Farms.

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:48.770
 And I just kind of came to visit on the farm and slowly, you know, got interested and started kind of learning about agriculture through him.

00:02:49.630 --> 00:03:01.380
 And, I mean, I think it's interesting, too, that Santa Cruz was a spot where, you know, we met, but we were also in our early years in college.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:09.850
 Like, that was a spot that opened both of us up to thinking in new ways about farming, thinking in new ways about food.

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:19.370
 It was, for me, it was a transformative time because of the peers that I surrounded myself with, because of the culture that existed at that time at that university.

00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:37.960
 And, you know, and so it was, it was definitely the, I went to UC Santa Cruz to get a degree in marine biology and very quickly, like, transform that into food systems and sustainable agriculture and spending time at the farm.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:04:03.290
 And when it became, you know, clear that there was an opportunity that I could actually farm based on some of the things that I learned in my time at Santa Cruz and some of the ideas that I'd been opened up to, but that I had the land, you know, that my parents had happened to have land, that, that's kind of become a canvas for a lot of the different things that I feel Katie and I have ended up collaborating on recently.

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:24.270
 So it was like, having land, having a piece of, you know, three or four acres of land to ground into every season and get creative and have that earthly connection, that was, yeah, that's something that really has become a jumping off board for Katie and I in our collaborative projects.

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:32.050
 And they've focused on agriculture in many ways, but, but, but beyond that too, into, into, you know, the culture that is a place.

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:44.060
 Well, maybe let's start there because I read, as I've been preparing for these podcasts, I've been reading about my friends online, which is like a pretty funny thing to be doing.

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:52.210
 And one of, one of the things I found, Pete, was an article about you and starting the farm as a thesis project, right?

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:55.390
 So it was like an experiment in sustainable business.

00:04:55.930 --> 00:05:03.380
 And it was to see if you could actually put all these theoretical things you were learning about into action.

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:11.270
 And so maybe you can, maybe you could explain what that was like to start that and what that looked like.

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:11.430
 Okay, sure.

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:21.070
 And I'll maybe even go, go backwards from, from where you led off of the question and start kind of right now and looking back to that.

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:38.620
 Cause that's been, um, been almost, almost a decade, basically, since I originally thought up the concept of writing my thesis about my, you know, 18 month experiment, experimental process, trying to grow vegetables at 5,000 feet in the Wasatch mountains for my community.

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:56.300
 And, and, and, and to look back at it, you know, now it's like, okay, I think I'm ready to revise that, that thesis and, and put in some, you know, experiential, um, knowledge that I've had back into that structure that I created about, you know, what it means to create a sustainable business through farming.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:18.640
 And I think that this has been the first year now to have a one-year-old son that, uh, the concepts of soil fertility and long-term soil care, um, you know, the concepts of seed saving, um, and microclimate creation through hoop houses and low tech, you know, solutions to altering growing space and climate.

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:41.390
 And, and, and what that does to your marketability, you know, all those things I've learned a huge, huge amount of since I came up with that idea of, of having my studies in college focus on, um, that question of how to sustain, you know, my livelihood and my family through, through farming, you know, on a piece of land on a yearly basis.

00:06:41.390 --> 00:06:56.980
 So, um, yeah, the, the, the idea for that thesis was pretty personal, you know, I think that's kind of how I've tried to make my education in my, in my life is like personalizing the, the experience and the, in the learning.

00:06:56.980 --> 00:07:13.540
 And for me, that was kind of like a, the thesis was kind of a recap on my life in a lot of ways, like reflecting on a little, all the different things that have influenced me to be passionate about what I happen to find myself passionate in now and why and where that comes from and, um, and how I can sustain it.

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:23.360
 That, you know, um, and that's what my thesis kind of became as a little honoring of all the friends and the teachers along the way that have kind of influenced me.

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:38.700
 And then, like I said, kind of the framework of, okay, you have four acres of land that you happen to buy or lease, inherit, trade for, so, you know, you come upon what, what are some of the main components for creating a small scale farm there?

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:46.190
 Um, and that's still the experiment that's happening a decade later, you know, but I'm, I'm, I'm ready for a revised edition.

00:07:46.450 --> 00:07:51.460
 Do you remember, I'm just curious, like what the question or the thesis statement was?

00:07:52.310 --> 00:07:58.500
 I guess I don't.

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:02.960
 Cause I, I mean, I guess for me that begs the question, like, does it work?

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:03.900
 Can you do it?

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:04.450
 Right.

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:05.160
 Yeah.

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:14.360
 I mean, the, the, the title was, was, uh, homegrown revolution, small scale vegetable production, um, uh, for the community.

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:17.920
 I think it wasn't that heavy on the business side, was it?

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:19.010
 No.

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:19.390
 No.

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:28.360
 Cause now at this point, like you was saying with a kid, I feel like that, um, really like crunching those numbers has become a reality.

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:39.230
 Like, is this possible to grow food and have this be our, like what sustains us and what enables us to live the lives that we want to live.

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:51.490
 And I think at that stage, it was more like a lot of idealism of Brown, just like the best way to live in harmony with, you know, um, your community and with nature.

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:56.050
 And now it's like, okay, and the money making end has become very real.

00:08:56.050 --> 00:09:08.220
 And so it's, I, I would say that that has been happening, but was, um, you know, uh, really, I would say the focus of this season more so than any other season is a financial piece.

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:16.480
 And I think that like, from my perspective, the ruling is not out yet on whether it is possible to really make a living.

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:26.870
 Pete is very convinced that it is a lot of people to prove about my, my theories of productivity on a, you know, per square foot basis on a one and a half acre farm.

00:09:26.870 --> 00:09:35.930
 But I also know that there's models out there now that I'm learning about that I'm really excited, um, exist, you know, to have role models in, in agriculture.

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:41.270
 I feel like I asked Katie, you know, like I'm up late night watching YouTube videos on JM.

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:42.890
 He's got a new hero.

