 It was like one person we'd visit a organic pony co just outside of Havana that gave us like directions in our, on our map of exactly the next organic pony co that we had to see and check out their like composting setup and like very specific, you know, networking of, of farmers.

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:26.340
 And, and so two things kind of fascinated me, I guess, about this trip in terms of Haiti and my relation and our collaboration was that for one, what did fascinate me about Cuba was the network of the farmer to farmer exchanges.

00:26:26.340 --> 00:26:46.800
 You know, the, um, you know, the, um, the, um, national organization for small scale farmers, uh, different entities that really their focus was on bringing farmers together to share skills and a horizontal platform, uh, from farmer to farmer sharing experiences.

00:26:47.300 --> 00:26:58.720
 Uh, that was something that really clipped to me that, wow, they got, that's, that's agriculture innovation figured out is creating the system for farmers.

00:26:58.740 --> 00:27:05.860
 Farmers to communicate with each other and share best practices and share inspirations and challenges and even equipment.

00:27:05.860 --> 00:27:16.560
 And, um, and the second part about that trip was seeing how Katie and I could work together.

00:27:16.560 --> 00:27:24.700
 You know, it was, it was our honeymoon, so it was fluid and it was fun and there weren't any real, um, important objectives that we were trying to accomplish.

00:27:25.200 --> 00:27:29.300
 But at the same time, like, like Katie said, I'm into the technical thing.

00:27:29.300 --> 00:27:34.900
 So we pull over on our bike and see something happening that's unique and I get the farmer kind of excited that we care and that we want to listen.

00:27:34.900 --> 00:27:41.560
 And then they invite us in for coffee and then we're immediately like staying in that community for the next week or something.

00:27:41.560 --> 00:27:52.430
 And I think, yeah, it is like something that's different than like me just approaching and saying like, oh, hi, I'm doing a film about, you know, agriculture in Cuba.

00:27:52.430 --> 00:27:59.670
 It's like people, especially like rural people and they, you know, they feel very timid and that's very uncomfortable and unfamiliar.

00:27:59.670 --> 00:28:10.030
 But when, you know, we pull up, I mean, obviously we're an oddity already, you know, um, two Americans pulling up on like a bright turquoise tandem bicycle filled with stuff.

00:28:10.030 --> 00:28:17.760
 So people in the towns were always fascinated by us, but then to hear like, oh, that he's a farmer and he's knowledgeable and he grows the same things.

00:28:17.760 --> 00:28:25.530
 It's like an immediate point of connection that like, um, you know, that you don't have, if you're just a journalist kind of approaching.

00:28:25.830 --> 00:28:34.650
 Uh, so I feel like that, um, it, there really is like a different person to person connection that happens and it's not, you know, it's not false.

00:28:34.650 --> 00:28:40.450
 Like Pete is sincerely so interested in what, um, people are doing in farming.

00:28:40.450 --> 00:28:47.890
 And like I said, I'm, I'm interested in that, but I'm also just interested to see how people are living their lives and how the communities work.

00:28:47.890 --> 00:29:09.680
 So we really, um, that's what I'm saying is that it's a, I sensed then that our partnership like had good balance, you know, uh, be because of all those reasons that you're just, you know, you're just saying there was like this, this kind of these different forms that we took when we're interacting with, with people that just, they work together, you know?

00:29:09.680 --> 00:29:32.080
 And yes, I had the agricultural interest in, in, inside, but also having a camera and a, you know, a microphone and headset and like having a five-year-old kid, like listen to the, you know, be the audio technician while they're doing the interview with his grandpa, you know, and you're like asking him questions and you're broken Spanish about like his toy truck or whatever.

00:29:32.080 --> 00:29:52.080
 It's like, there's multiple layers of connecting that, that happened or that happened, you know, that was, I think enabled some of the work that we did, you know, to, to be enhanced too, is because we, we were able to create kind of a well-rounded, um, relation with the people that we were interested in learning from.

00:29:52.280 --> 00:30:06.070
 You know, and from an audience or an audience and a friend perspective, it's like, that's a thing that I can see really follow through in your relationship is that, um, you find that balancing point with each other quite often.

00:30:06.250 --> 00:30:11.180
 And oftentimes it's around food and land and people talking about places.

00:30:11.520 --> 00:30:25.810
 And, um, I know that as you, I'd love to hear more about the film and hear about how that happened, but I know that as you worked on that, uh, Pete played like a plethora of roles while Katie,

00:30:25.810 --> 00:30:37.840
 while that was really Katie's project and her, her vision there, um, but that you, you have managed throughout your collaboration to kind of insert yourselves into each other's projects.

00:30:38.160 --> 00:30:44.130
 I'm sure sometimes in ways that don't work, but lots of times in ways that seem like they work really well.

00:30:44.130 --> 00:30:48.630
 And that I think that's what long-term collaboration actually looks like.

00:30:48.630 --> 00:30:50.560
 It's never equal, right?

00:30:50.640 --> 00:30:52.260
 And there's always this unevenness.

00:30:52.320 --> 00:30:55.880
 You're always navigating that point of engagement with each other.

00:30:56.060 --> 00:31:04.690
 So maybe let's talk a little bit about Guajiros and Katie, that was your, that was your master's thesis.

00:31:04.690 --> 00:31:08.570
 We're talking a lot about the thesis on this podcast.

00:31:09.930 --> 00:31:26.090
 Yeah, that, um, so, you know, coming back from our experience in Cuba and we actually went back, um, two more times and continue to, you know, kind of just talk to farmers in kind of an informal way.

00:31:26.090 --> 00:31:38.650
 And I thought that, um, at that time I was just doing photography and I really felt like, um, so inspired by hearing the actual words that people said and really seeing movement.

00:31:38.650 --> 00:31:40.510
 And I really wanted to branch into film.

00:31:40.610 --> 00:31:47.770
 And I also felt like I needed the support and structure of some other kind of entity in order to really execute that.

00:31:47.870 --> 00:31:51.030
 I felt like I had kind of reached the limits of what we could do on our own.

00:31:51.030 --> 00:31:57.650
 So, um, the graduate program at UC Santa Cruz in social documentation sort of form that structure for me.

00:31:57.870 --> 00:32:02.510
 And, um, we had already been collecting and building a lot of relationships with farmers.

00:32:02.510 --> 00:32:08.380
 So, it felt natural to go back and do a project about agriculture in Cuba.

00:32:08.890 --> 00:32:18.310
 And as we started to develop that idea, um, he had the opportunity to travel down for a, um, delegation.

00:32:18.980 --> 00:32:20.380
 What was the name of that delegation?

00:32:20.800 --> 00:32:28.990
 It was the third or fourth annual agroecology, national agroecology delegation in Cuba.

00:32:29.710 --> 00:32:33.100
 And on that trip, do you want to talk about meeting Ernst?

00:32:33.310 --> 00:32:34.630
 Sure, yeah.

00:32:35.460 --> 00:32:36.300
 Way back up.

00:32:36.500 --> 00:32:37.120
 Why'd you go?

00:32:38.030 --> 00:32:47.860
 Yeah, um, I went because, um, well, I knew I was kind of in a, the timing of it, I was in a transition period,

00:32:47.860 --> 00:32:57.230
 almost like I'm entering into now of between ending a farm season, searching for winter, winter work,

00:32:57.890 --> 00:33:04.810
 being excited about the whole season of farming that I just kind of wrapped up and put to bed under a blanket of mulch

00:33:04.810 --> 00:33:07.430
 and to say goodnight until the following spring.

00:33:08.220 --> 00:33:11.370
 And, you know, we had just, we had just, um...

00:33:11.370 --> 00:33:13.230
 I had just started my program.

00:33:13.330 --> 00:33:26.740
 Katie had just started her program and we had been talking about, you know, how do we, how do we shape and, and mold our, our, the stories that we want to tell in Cuba into Katie's thesis project.

00:33:26.740 --> 00:33:31.340
 And, and I think the plan was originally that we were both going to go.

00:33:31.490 --> 00:33:32.390
 Yeah.

00:33:32.590 --> 00:33:37.410
 And, and, and the timing didn't line up with, with school quarters and, and stuff like that.

00:33:37.430 --> 00:33:41.820
 And so I ended up going as a representative of the team.

00:33:42.460 --> 00:33:50.750
 And, um, yeah, it was, I think there was 38 or 39 countries represented from Latin America and the Caribbean.

00:33:50.750 --> 00:34:01.670
 And, um, and yeah, I think it was just one of the second or third days I got to talking with this man, uh, Ernst Jean-Baptiste.

00:34:01.930 --> 00:34:05.350
 And, and we just got talking about why we're in Cuba.

00:34:05.490 --> 00:34:13.200
 And he was explaining to me that he had just started, um, a four-year program at the Agrarian University of Havana focused in agroecology.

00:34:14.110 --> 00:34:17.640
 And, um, yes, I think it was his first year.

00:34:18.190 --> 00:34:19.820
 And really excited.

00:34:19.920 --> 00:34:33.630
 All he could talk about was wanting to get back to Haiti as soon as he could to share what he's been learning about food production on a small scale without chemicals and without mechanized machinery.

00:34:34.690 --> 00:34:39.720
 And we just started talking and I told him about, you know, the fact that Katie and I had spent time in Cuba.

00:34:39.720 --> 00:34:53.870
 We were really passionate as well about sharing the story, um, of Cuba's kind of transformation in food production with other farmers and other growers and other people in, in the States.

00:34:53.950 --> 00:35:00.050
 And he, I remember he said something like, well, come and, you know, come and tell my, tell my story.

00:35:00.050 --> 00:35:03.410
 And so that's, I mean, that was like the very first kind of contact that we had.

00:35:03.490 --> 00:35:08.350
 And we kept in touch for almost a year and a half before we actually saw him again.

00:35:09.470 --> 00:35:15.110
 Yeah. Well, and so Pete came back from that delegation and he was talking about all different kinds of things.

00:35:15.210 --> 00:35:34.060
 They had visited all these farms and, you know, just kind of casually mentioning errands to, he had met and, um, you know, just had really clicked with as a friend and, you know, um, talking about him, you know, the project that he wanted to start back in Haiti and that he was studying there.

00:35:34.240 --> 00:35:55.320
 And something just kind of clicked for me that this was, um, um, a story and a character that I thought would be really powerful for, um, showing some of the, um, you know, showing some of the points about what Cuba is doing that I found to be most important.

00:35:55.320 --> 00:36:19.500
 And to really show it through this different kind of lens, because something about Cuba is that a lot of people look at Cuba under a microscope and sort of feel like what happens there is an anomaly because it's been so isolated and has had kind of such a unique social, cultural, historical, um, place that what happens there can't be applied anywhere else.

00:36:19.500 --> 00:36:29.850
 So I was really interested in, like, what, what does Cuba, what can we learn from Cuba and what potential does that have for other places and particularly other third world countries?

00:36:30.540 --> 00:36:44.940
 Um, and so I thought that, um, the idea that, you know, a lot of people know about Cuba's internationalist policies in healthcare, where they educate people from all over the world, doctors and send them out.

00:36:44.940 --> 00:36:50.060
 But people don't really know that Cuba is doing that in other areas such as sustainable agriculture.

00:36:50.260 --> 00:36:53.630
 There are people from hundreds of different countries that are training there.

00:36:53.830 --> 00:37:09.400
 And in the agrarian university, um, we met people from all over the world from, you know, um, you know, just countries throughout Latin America, Africa, Asia, North America.

00:37:09.400 --> 00:37:21.250
 And everybody there was really focused on a particular aspect of, um, of agriculture that they felt would help their communities in some way.

00:37:21.350 --> 00:37:23.010
 Very specific topics, too.

00:37:23.130 --> 00:37:32.970
 Like, this one woman was studying integrated pest management for a certain, um, larvae that infected corn crops in her...

00:37:32.970 --> 00:37:33.110
 In La Soto.

00:37:33.230 --> 00:37:33.770
 In La Soto.

00:37:34.110 --> 00:37:34.310
 Yeah.

00:37:34.620 --> 00:37:48.730
 And she was, like, figuring out, learning, her thesis was focused on everything she could possibly learn about organic approaches to controlling different, you know, illnesses or, um, insect, you know, issues that you can have in corn.

00:37:49.310 --> 00:38:15.280
 And, and, and yeah, like Katie was saying, they were all very specific to not just going to a university and learning about farming or learning about sustainable farming, but learning about specific agroecological, organic, as much as possible approaches to specific issues that they could bring that education back home and have an impact on, or at least work with others that can create a plan for something that will, would change, you know, really fascinating.

00:38:15.280 --> 00:38:32.190
 And so in Aaron's case, he's from Jacques Mel, which has historically been, um, a tourist part of Haiti and, um, you know, had some, he was actually in Cuba when the earthquake hit.

00:38:32.540 --> 00:38:44.310
 Um, so Jacques Mel felt a lot of the ripples of the earthquake, um, literally, and, you know, just, um, was devastated in different ways.

00:38:44.610 --> 00:39:03.840
 So, um, he really wanted to focus on creating some kind of program and really introducing sustainable agriculture to his community, but tying in tourism in some way, um, to make that economically viable and really bring the youth in.

00:39:04.000 --> 00:39:12.720
 And, um, like a lot has been lost in agriculture, um, in Haiti due to, uh, big business and foreign aid.

00:39:12.720 --> 00:39:28.640
 And so he really was in a mindset of like, um, you know, bring Haiti back to the Haitians and, you know, really wanted like farmers to lead the way and, um, and growing their own food there.

00:39:28.800 --> 00:39:36.760
 So he was a really interesting character to me and I had never met him, but we talked on Skype and, you know, shared emails.

00:39:36.760 --> 00:39:39.320
 And he said, yes, I would definitely want to be part of the film.

00:39:39.320 --> 00:39:47.700
 And we lined up all these things and we, you know, um, my program was very character driven in terms of documentary.

00:39:47.700 --> 00:39:50.040
 So I really planned to make him my central character.

00:39:50.880 --> 00:39:59.180
 And, uh, we landed in Havana, called him on the phone, couldn't get through, phone doesn't exist.

00:39:59.440 --> 00:40:02.600
 Day two, couldn't get through, phone doesn't exist, called the university.

00:40:03.430 --> 00:40:11.450
 Finally, like head out to, um, San Jose de las Lajas, which is about like an hour outside of Havana to the Ukrainian university.

00:40:11.450 --> 00:40:19.080
 And we're just kind of like, do you know any Haitians like to the guard at the university gate?

00:40:19.140 --> 00:40:21.240
 And he's like, oh yeah, all the Haitians live in that dorm.

00:40:21.700 --> 00:40:27.100
 And sure enough, we, you know, someone walks down the hall, like, you know, Ernst, and we get to his door.

00:40:27.140 --> 00:40:30.630
 He's like, I've been waiting for you.

00:40:30.690 --> 00:40:32.330
 And we're like, your phone is dead.

00:40:32.370 --> 00:40:33.150
 Where have you been?

00:40:33.230 --> 00:40:34.570
 We're here with all this equipment.

00:40:34.850 --> 00:40:39.510
 And we had, um, you know, a bunch of obstacles in getting off the ground with that.

00:40:39.510 --> 00:40:45.050
 And, um, and so Welbury was his roommate also from Haiti, from, um, Capetia in a different region.

00:40:45.510 --> 00:40:49.550
 And he was like, had his own very interesting story.

00:40:49.910 --> 00:40:56.050
 And, um, they both, uh, were just about to graduate from this program.

00:40:56.050 --> 00:41:02.130
 And we're really thinking about, you know, what, how they were going to take their education back to Haiti.

00:41:02.490 --> 00:41:11.310
 So at that time we were, we traveled all over the island and, um, you know, with Ernst and Welbury and talked to farmers.

00:41:11.310 --> 00:41:15.670
 And they were really idealistic about bringing these ideas from Cuba back to Haiti.

00:41:16.090 --> 00:41:20.500
 And my plan was to continue the documentary in Haiti.

00:41:20.760 --> 00:41:22.780
 And I still would like to do a follow-up with them.

00:41:22.880 --> 00:41:26.700
 We're in touch quite a bit, but, um, we haven't filmed with them.

00:41:26.700 --> 00:41:32.110
 But really it was about, you know, the Cuba part was a lot of idealism.

00:41:32.390 --> 00:41:35.370
 And then returning to Haiti has been a lot of reality check.

00:41:35.490 --> 00:41:40.340
 And really, um, you know, they faced a lot of challenges in really trying to launch their projects.

00:41:40.620 --> 00:41:53.240
 And, um, get things going in terms of, um, you know, some of the farmer training programs and inspiration from this, like, farmer-to-farmer model that they really want to help implement in Haiti.

00:41:53.460 --> 00:41:55.480
 So it's been a couple years.

00:41:55.480 --> 00:42:00.100
 And, um, like I said, I would love to really do an update on the film.

00:42:00.220 --> 00:42:05.350
 But that's kind of the story of the, you know, at least initially of the documentary.

00:42:06.790 --> 00:42:10.390
 There's a lot of different directions we could go with talking about that.

00:42:10.790 --> 00:42:23.910
 Um, I really want to hear more about this time with the farm and what you guys are envisioning for the next few things in your life and where you see collaboration happening.

00:42:23.910 --> 00:42:31.490
 And, um, for some reason, like, farmer-to-farmer stories feel really involved with that to me.

00:42:31.570 --> 00:42:38.110
 I know, Katie, you're going tonight to film something about farm workers at Pie Ranch and just up Highway 1.

00:42:38.370 --> 00:42:43.030
 And maybe you're back in Santa Cruz for four days.

00:42:43.190 --> 00:42:45.830
 Maybe we can talk about what's next.

00:42:46.230 --> 00:43:08.900
 Yeah, I mean, I think it's definitely, like, that's a question we're kind of asking at this seaside table right here in our dining room as well is, like, you know, I think that for me, this farm season, you know, is challenging in many ways and educational in many more ways.

00:43:08.900 --> 00:43:21.450
 And, um, and it's still, it's still happening, you know, I've got 90,000 more clothes to plant before the snow falls and plenty more winter squash and potatoes to pull.

00:43:21.610 --> 00:43:30.470
 So my mind is still kind of in the logistical, um, and financial realms of the farm season.

00:43:30.710 --> 00:43:32.150
 And you fly back there tonight.

00:43:32.410 --> 00:43:33.250
 I'm driving, yeah.

00:43:33.250 --> 00:43:34.190
 Oh, you drive back tonight.

00:43:34.310 --> 00:43:36.590
 Through the desert with, with, uh, brother John Moran.

00:43:36.590 --> 00:43:43.110
 And so we're going to have a, you know, a good, good adventure out there and, and get some good harvesting done right away when we land.

00:43:43.250 --> 00:44:02.020
 But, um, yeah, so I'm kind of in that transition time, like I said, of like reflecting on what the farm season has been for us as, as a couple and as a family now and, and individually and for the farm and the business, like kind of reflecting on what this 2015 season has been.

00:44:03.820 --> 00:44:09.370
 And, and, and then starting that creative process of like, okay, how do we apply that's every year?

00:44:09.450 --> 00:44:10.110
 That's what it is.

00:44:10.110 --> 00:44:24.310
 It's like how you apply the, the magic part, you know, the part that really was efficient and, and flowing and, and economically viable and all those things, how to apply those seamlessly into the start of the next growing season.

00:44:24.310 --> 00:44:35.870
 And so for me, that's what the winter is, is like, um, you know, organization equals success, you know, like that's kind of where my, my mind is in terms of the farm creatively.

00:44:36.320 --> 00:44:42.900
 Um, but yeah, it's, it's like that, that exciting time to reflect and then get creative again.

00:44:42.900 --> 00:44:47.060
 And I think that for me, and I can't help it.

00:44:47.060 --> 00:44:59.910
 That's it in the Wasatch mountains, you know, in Utah, and I'm open to it being in another place, even though my heart, I can't lie as is connected there to those, you know, rural Utah, Northern mountains.

00:45:01.180 --> 00:45:05.180
 Um, I want to keep figuring out how I can farm to support my family.

00:45:05.730 --> 00:45:07.430
 You know, that's, it's what I love to do.

00:45:07.470 --> 00:45:13.310
 It's what makes me feel healthy and makes me feel like I'm excited to do it the next day and the next month and the next year.

00:45:13.310 --> 00:45:30.530
 So I'm going to continue getting, focusing on that financial side, you know, and that business plan side and, and get serious about the numbers so that the excitement and the enjoyment of the rest of what farming is can unfold, you know, as it needs to for the growing season.

00:45:30.530 --> 00:45:44.870
 And then for our collaboration, you know, I think that, I think that there's a lot of directions that it can go, but one that I think we're both excited about is the idea of, of having a site, a location where we can host.

00:45:44.890 --> 00:46:08.330
 Um, gatherings of, of, of, of young farmers, small scale farmers, international farmers, farmers that can come and tell stories and be, have their stories heard and recorded in unique ways and, um, and have outlets for, you know, for them to interact with the community that they're visiting, um, and have, and have some kind of exchange, um, be possible.

00:46:08.330 --> 00:46:16.090
 And, and, and that's still taking shape, you know, and still in the, in the visioning and dreaming stage.

00:46:16.090 --> 00:46:33.370
 But, um, I know that that's, that people to people sharing of knowledge and information and, and park garden, you know, stories, that's the part that I know is connects us both to what we love about food and farming, you know, so.

00:46:33.370 --> 00:46:50.640
 Well, and I think that, um, this sort of, um, tension that currently exists, um, that you may hear is just that rural Utah is not where I want to be.

00:46:50.640 --> 00:47:10.500
 I love the farm and, um, I love so many things about it, but as I said, I'm really so fascinated by culture and I really thrive on, um, diversity and, um, and just community.

00:47:10.500 --> 00:47:16.040
 And so where the farm is located happens to be in a very rural Mormon Valley.

00:47:16.320 --> 00:47:22.200
 And I find that I don't have the same connection to that place that I have in other places.

00:47:22.500 --> 00:47:35.360
 So we've, that's kind of been, um, something for us that we're trying to figure out is how can we be there on the farm, but still have these, you know, pieces that really stimulate us.

00:47:35.360 --> 00:48:07.010
 And, you know, and also are like, it's not totally fulfilling to just farm very high end organic food for an elite customer, but to really somehow, um, do that farming work and have it have, um, a larger impact and, um, you know, participate in a project that really, um, is working towards, you know,

00:48:07.010 --> 00:48:22.600
 bringing, like making healthy food accessible to people and also like, um, spreading knowledge of sustainable agriculture to people in places where maybe, um, it's not at this time.

00:48:22.600 --> 00:48:37.790
 So I think that the idea of potentially, um, we've made quite a few amazing connections around the world, um, and really have talked about, um, doing like a farmer to farmer exchange and having that land.

00:48:37.790 --> 00:48:40.050
 And be, um, um, be, um, a site for that.

00:48:40.050 --> 00:49:04.570
 And so that's something that excites me and potentially bringing, um, you know, a documentary piece into that, um, process and really like having people, um, share their stories and, you know, um, working with youth and, you know, we already work with several youth groups in that area that do media projects and things.

00:49:04.570 --> 00:49:10.650
 So I feel like that's the piece where I want to tie in, but yeah, we're just kind of figuring that out.

00:49:10.650 --> 00:49:24.310
 And, um, um, like, as I was telling you earlier, I feel like the experience of our lives so far has really been like to have the clarity of purpose and project.

00:49:24.710 --> 00:49:36.330
 And then it really feels like, um, um, we can achieve it, but when, you know, we don't have that piece of clarity, then it's harder.

00:49:36.590 --> 00:49:44.950
 And so I think that right now, Pete has a very clear vision for the farm and he's very confident and passionate about what he's doing at Sand Hill Farms.

00:49:44.950 --> 00:50:01.950
 And so I, as in our partnership, I just want to encourage him in that and I'm working to kind of find my clarity and like stay true to my vision and my dreams in places where they intersect with his and then places where maybe they don't.

00:50:02.430 --> 00:50:19.480
 So that's sort of the moment that you find us in, um, you know, as Pete is headed back to the farm for a week and I'm going to be here in Santa Cruz with the baby and just kind of like figuring out what the next, um, chapter looks like.

00:50:19.540 --> 00:50:20.260
 But it's exciting.

00:50:20.520 --> 00:50:23.180
 It does feel exciting and that there's a lot of potential.

